This is the bug. [vm-esp32s3_i2s.c:1145](C:/Users/VICTUS/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/c--Users-VICTUS-openVela/3659500b-38f3-47db-bbd0-9b2aab159cd9/scratchpad/vm-esp32s3_i2s.c#L1145) — the RX completion handler only advances the queue if the descriptor the hardware reports matches the one it walked to:

```c
bfdesc = bfcontainer->dma_link;
while (bfdesc->next != NULL && (bfdesc->next->ctrl & ESP32S3_DMA_CTRL_EOF))
    bfdesc = bfdesc->next;

if (bfdesc == inlink)          /* <-- no else branch */
  {
    sq_remfirst(&priv->rx.act);
    ...
    if (sq_empty(&priv->rx.act))
        i2s_rxdma_start(priv);
  }
```

**There is no `else`.** On a mismatch the container stays in `rx.act`, and `i2s_rxdma_start()` bails at its own first guard:

```c
if (!sq_empty(&priv->rx.act))
    return OK;         /* "already active" — but nothing is active */
```

RX is then wedged permanently. Every subsequent buffer we enqueue lands in `rx.pend` and is never loaded — which is precisely `rec 1/19`: one completion got through, the second mismatched, and the remaining 18 sat in `pend` forever. es8311's worker then can't terminate with buffers outstanding, so `AUDIOIOC_STOP` joins it and hangs at **stage 34**. Every symptom falls out of this one missing branch.

And it ties directly to that descriptor mismatch already sitting in your backlog — `eofnum=0x0ffc` against `d[0]=0xff0` + `d[1]=0x10`. EOF is programmed to fire at 4092 bytes while the descriptor chain spans 4096 across two descriptors, so the EOF lands *inside* `d[1]`. Whether `bfdesc` walks to `d[1]` depends on `d[1]`'s EOF ctrl bit. That's the intermittency: same binary, different luck.

Let me confirm where `nbytes` and the descriptor EOF bits are set before I propose a fix.

